{"title":"Polynomial Remainder Theorem","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><p>In <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Algebra\" title=\"Algebra\">algebra</a>, the <b>Polynomial Remainder Theorem</b> states that the remainder upon <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Synthetic_division\" title=\"Synthetic division\"> dividing</a> any <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial\" title=\"Polynomial\">polynomial</a> <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> by a linear polynomial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/b/79b2b7ab0341df3dd3ea02ee6680ae31a947af07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-a$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" />, both with <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complex_number\" title=\"Complex number\">complex</a> coefficients, is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/a/f2ac37e8ff9aeea3ac5eaf42779864032eb645c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" />.\n</p>\n<div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Proof\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Proof</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Generalization\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Generalization</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Problems\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problems</span></a>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Example_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Example 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-2 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#More_Problems\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3.2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">More Problems</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Proof\">Proof</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial_Remainder_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Proof\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>By polynomial division with dividend <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> and divisor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/b/79b2b7ab0341df3dd3ea02ee6680ae31a947af07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-a$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" />, that exist a quotient <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/d/d9dfe7c7a0b887097fa2227a230fc915ba2e5477.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> and remainder <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/e/a3eeefa367883375994fd2e1f575d088bce9d8b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" /> such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/3/0b321a4b44189ac437193749edc4786d10a9b7ed.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[P(x) = (x-a) Q(x) + R(x)\\]\" width=\"217\" height=\"18\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/2/fe251e6bc7af7ae8ed9f967728f94b07c45140a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\deg R(x) &lt; \\deg (x-a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"179\" height=\"20\" />. We wish to show that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/e/a3eeefa367883375994fd2e1f575d088bce9d8b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" /> is equal to the constant <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/a/f2ac37e8ff9aeea3ac5eaf42779864032eb645c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" />. Because <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/8/ff8e51946cf0a440bc3a35b6f68cbddc889b8e03.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\deg (x-a) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"119\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/a/1/4a1e530c3a773504921279a6cb4b8b104cf9bda5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\deg R(x) &lt; 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"20\" />. Therefore, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/f/0/3f058056ca4acce50bfa421bf06673007b62e64a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\deg R(x) = 0$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"104\" height=\"20\" />, and so the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/e/a3eeefa367883375994fd2e1f575d088bce9d8b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" /> is a constant.\n</p><p>Let this constant be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />. We may substitute this into our original equation and rearrange to yield <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/b/8/4b8a2ee539104889461066a33875deae2201bd2b.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[r = P(x) - (x-a) Q(x).\\]\" width=\"192\" height=\"18\" /> When <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/7/7/e77ba6189a32e5ead2cbb44585ede2285792545c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x = a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"46\" height=\"10\" />, this equation becomes <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/0/0706c2ed14ecb3cc89058594e9d184ff9d7bcd11.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r = P(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"73\" height=\"20\" />. Hence, the remainder upon dividing <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/b/79b2b7ab0341df3dd3ea02ee6680ae31a947af07.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-a$\" width=\"42\" height=\"8\" /> is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/a/f2ac37e8ff9aeea3ac5eaf42779864032eb645c9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"36\" height=\"18\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Generalization\">Generalization</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial_Remainder_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Generalization\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>The strategy used in the above proof can be generalized to divisors with degree greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. A more general method, with any dividend <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/f/6efaaf74e576202fad965134bc49c6a34aac4c30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> and divisor <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/2/8a2040e00c5533e3da9f83349e48b5fb00ef920a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" />, is to write <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/7/da78c6c2d2a4530d9a8b549b75770568044811c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R(x) = D(x) Q(x) - P(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"205\" height=\"20\" />, and then substitute the zeroes of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/a/2/8a2040e00c5533e3da9f83349e48b5fb00ef920a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"42\" height=\"20\" /> to eliminate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/d/d9dfe7c7a0b887097fa2227a230fc915ba2e5477.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"41\" height=\"20\" /> and find values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/e/a3eeefa367883375994fd2e1f575d088bce9d8b1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R(x)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"40\" height=\"20\" />. Example 2 showcases this strategy.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problems\">Problems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial_Remainder_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Problems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Here are some problems with solutions that utilize the Polynomial Remainder Theorem and its generalization.\n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Example_1\">Example 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial_Remainder_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Example 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<p><i>What is the remainder when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/e/6ce9e6877b35c3c1ab377e96ac005d4d117bd3ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x^2+2x+3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"90\" height=\"16\" /> is divided by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/c/cccb103143393c0119f5ab61d6d53a852ce70fcf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"44\" height=\"15\" />?</i>\n</p><p><b>Solution</b>: Although one could use long or synthetic division, the Polynomial Remainder Theorem provides a significantly shorter solution. Note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/f/c/2fc5bff9e94b78e6759a518b90313d6984fd02fa.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(x) = x^2 + 2x + 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"156\" height=\"21\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/9/8/a98ed7ed8e3c22f6c49f128985b07a4955b41985.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x-a = x+1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"110\" height=\"15\" />. A common mistake is to forget to flip the negative sign and assume <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/d/48d02444a59b917b64e8bd0963b88712fc7fa0a3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />, but simplifying the linear equation yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/4/35496101e151991fc3213a7472ade63fd75bdf8e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a = -1$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"56\" height=\"12\" />. Thus, the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/9/9/b99e47e493866342e4bf09b94942f79845cbc5c6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P(-1)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"49\" height=\"18\" />, or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/3/b73339cb481f5dc726dea43d604fee766ad767b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(-1)^2 + 2(-1) + 3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"147\" height=\"21\" />, which is equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/c/b6cf65b1f2fbdf388e3daeff9b96b34d3399d777.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\square$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />. \n</p>\n<h3><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"More_Problems\">More Problems</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial_Remainder_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: More Problems\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h3>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1950_AHSME_Problems/Problem_20\" title=\"1950 AHSME Problems/Problem 20\">1950 AHSME Problem 20</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1961_AHSME_Problems/Problem_22\" title=\"1961 AHSME Problems/Problem 22\">1961 AHSME Problem 22</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=1969_AHSME_Problems/Problem_34\" title=\"1969 AHSME Problems/Problem 34\">1969 AHSME Problem 34</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_7\" title=\"2025 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 7\">2025 AMC 10A Problem 7</a></li></ul>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_Also\">See Also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial_Remainder_Theorem&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: See Also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<ul><li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial\" title=\"Polynomial\">Polynomial</a></li>\n<li><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Factor_theorem\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"Factor theorem\">Factor theorem</a></li></ul>\n<p><i>This article is a stub.  Help us out by <span class=\"plainlinks\"><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"https://artofproblemsolving.com/wiki/index.php?title=Polynomial_Remainder_Theorem&amp;action=edit\">expanding it</a></span>.</i>\n</p>\n<!-- \nNewPP limit report\nCached time: 20261017210856\nCache expiry: 86400\nReduced expiry: false\nComplications: [show‐toc]\nCPU time usage: 0.023 seconds\nReal time usage: 0.033 seconds\nPreprocessor visited node count: 232/1000000\nPost‐expand include size: 325/2097152 bytes\nTemplate argument size: 0/2097152 bytes\nHighest expansion depth: 4/100\nExpensive parser function count: 0/100\nUnstrip recursion depth: 0/20\nUnstrip post‐expand size: 6991/5000000 bytes\n-->\n<!--\nTransclusion expansion time report (%,ms,calls,template)\n100.00%    5.343      1 Template:Stub\n100.00%    5.343      1 -total\n-->\n\n<!-- Saved in parser cache with key wiki-wiki_:pcache:15647:|#|:idhash:canonical and timestamp 20261017210856 and revision id 292412. Rendering was triggered because: page-view\n -->\n</div>"}